<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Cornouaille</h1><p class="st-meta">500 CE – 850 CE (approx.) · capital Quimper</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Breton kingdom of south-western Brittany, founded by settlers from Britain and centred on Quimper.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>From the 5th and 6th centuries migrants from western <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> settled the Armorican peninsula, once the land of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/osismii">Osismii</a>. Those in the south-west named their territory after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a>: Kernev in Breton, Cornubia in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>. Its early history is known mainly from saints' lives and legends, such as that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gradlon">King Gradlon</a> and the drowned city of Ys.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The kingdom was ruled by local Breton dynasties and organised around monasteries and the see of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quimper">Quimper</a>, traditionally founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-corentin">Saint Corentin</a>. The abbey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landevennec-abbey">Landévennec</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winwaloe">Saint Guénolé</a>, was its chief religious centre and produced important <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the 9th century Cornouaille was drawn into the unified <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-brittany">Kingdom of Brittany</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nominoe">Nominoe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erispoe">Erispoe</a>. It survived as a county whose counts later became dukes of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-brittany">Brittany</a> in the 11th century, and the name remains in use for the region around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quimper">Quimper</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/cornouaille.jpg" alt="Cornouaille" width="320" height="245"><figcaption>Image: Mikael Bodlore-Penlaez · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map-Bro-Gernev.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>500 CE – 850 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Quimper</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Celtic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Breton</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kernev, Cornubia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=675&amp;focus=cornouaille" aria-label="Cornouaille on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4585 497 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Cornouaille lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4905 753L4930 753"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4930 753h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4905 753h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4905 753h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Quimper. <a href="/atlas?year=675&amp;focus=cornouaille">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
